# Formula (3.23): beta1-tilde = beta1-hat + beta2-hat * delta1-tilde. The simple regression of Y on X1 gives beta1-tilde = 1.17. The multiple regression of Y on X1 and X2 gives beta2-hat = -0.94, and the slope from regressing X2 on X1 is delta1-tilde = 0.47. Find the multiple-regression slope beta1-hat. (round to the hundredths)
# Hint: solve (3.23) for beta1-hat: beta1-hat = beta1-tilde - beta2-hat * delta1-tilde. Watch the signs.
b1_tilde <- 1.17    # edit
b2_hat   <- -0.94   # edit
d1_tilde <- 0.47    # edit
round(b1_tilde - b2_hat * d1_tilde, 2)   # 1.61
# Opposite direction (given beta1-hat, find the simple slope beta1-tilde):
#   b1_hat <- 1.61; round(b1_hat + b2_hat * d1_tilde, 2)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data: bwght (bwght = a baby's birth weight in ounces; cigs = cigarettes the mother smoked per day during pregnancy; faminc = the family's income in thousands of dollars). Regress faminc (the variable left out of the simple regression in problem 12) on cigs, lm(faminc ~ cigs, data = bwght), to get delta1-tilde. Compute beta2-hat * delta1-tilde, the faminc coefficient from the multiple regression times this slope. (round to the hundredths)
# Hint: by (3.23), beta2-hat * delta1-tilde = beta1-tilde - beta1-hat: the simple-regression cigs slope minus the multiple-regression one. Use that to check your answer.
library(wooldridge)
data("bwght")
simple   <- lm(bwght ~ cigs, data = bwght)
multiple <- lm(bwght ~ cigs + faminc, data = bwght)
aux      <- lm(faminc ~ cigs, data = bwght)
round(unname(coef(multiple)["faminc"] * coef(aux)["cigs"]), 2)        # -0.05
round(unname(coef(simple)["cigs"] - coef(multiple)["cigs"]), 2)       # -0.05 (check)
# For reference: simple cigs slope = -0.51, multiple cigs slope = -0.46

